, "money": -0.69}, "rating_margin": -6.9, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.2, "baseline_margin": -2.91, "analyst_adjustment": -1.09, "key_factors": ["District's Republican lean (R+7 PVI) in a redrawn seat Figures won narrowly", "Expert consensus of Likely/Lean R flip, with markets pricing Democrats near 27%", "Available polls (all Democratic-sponsored, last from August) show a dead heat", "Figures holds a cash advantage while Democratic outside groups outspend Republicans on ads"], "rationale": "The seat's Republican fundamentals and a broad rater consensus pointing to a GOP flip outweigh Figures' incumbency, fundraising edge, and the tied summer polling, which is thin, dated, and Democratic-sponsored. Prediction markets and most raters still favor Marques, though the race is competitive enough to stay short of Likely R.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling and October ad spending in the Mobile-area swing county could shift the race toward either candidate.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Shomari Figures (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Alabama's 2nd District is a newly drawn, Republican-leaning seat held by Democratic incumbent Shomari Figures, who won it narrowly and is now defending against Republican nominee Rhett Marques. Most race raters call the contest a Likely or Lean Republican flip, though a few see it as closer, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-four chance. The district's partisan lean works against Figures even with incumbency.\n\nThe limited public polling, all sponsored by Democratic groups and dating from June through August, showed the candidates essentially tied. Figures has slightly outraised Marques and holds more cash on hand, and Democratic outside groups are outspending Republican ones on television, while Trump has campaigned to boost Republican turnout. The race remains genuinely competitive but tilts Republican.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2955, "margin": -3.05}, {"date": "2026-09